\chapter{Console, Terminals and Clock}
\label{ch:console}

The x86 version has a VGA screen and a keyboard with ten virtual terminals. Here there is one UART. The port keeps the
terminals of the book as buffers, switches between them with Ctrl-T and a digit, and translates the screen functions of the book
(\texttt{set\_xy}, \texttt{clrscr}, \texttt{write\_screen}) into ANSI sequences, so that \texttt{vi} works. The clock reads the
PL031 RTC if the board has one; the Raspberry Pi has none, and time then starts at a fixed date.

@
\section{The console: keyboard input comes from the PL011 UART instead of the PS/2 keyboard}
\label{src:arch_console.c}

On the [[x86]] the keyboard interrupt handler turns scan codes into characters and stores them in the
input buffer of the current virtual terminal (terminals[[[cur_vt]]]); processes that wait for a key sit
in [[keyboard_queue]].  Here the UART receive interrupt does the same job; everything behind the
buffer (kgetch, [[syscall_readchar]], kreadline, the kernel shell) is the code from the book.

  Enter          -> '\textbackslash{}n'                (terminals send CR)
  Backspace/DEL  -> '\textbackslash{}b'
  Ctrl-C         -> kill the foreground process of the terminal (like the [[x86]] keyboard handler)
  Ctrl-]         -> stop the scheduler and enter the kernel shell ([[x86]]: Shift-Esc)

